SOC Incident Report
Introduction to Phishing


1. Executive Summary
On 04 August 2026, a firewall alert was generated after an internal computer attempted to access a blacklisted external URL. I investigated the alert using Kibana by reviewing firewall logs and searching for related activity from the same source IP address. The investigation showed that the user carried out a normal Google search before attempting to access a Bit.ly shortened link that was blocked by the firewall. No additional suspicious activity or evidence of compromise was identified. I classified the incident as a True Positive with a Low severity rating because the firewall successfully prevented the connection.

2. Incident Details
Date and Time: 04 August 2026 14:53:52
Alert Source: Firewall
Alert Type: Blocked Website
Datasource: Firewall
Affected Asset: 10.20.2.17
Destination IP: 67.199.248.11
URL: http://bit.ly/3sHkX3da12340
Action: Blocked
Rule: Blocked Websites

3. Investigation Timeline
I started by reviewing the original firewall alert in Kibana. The alert showed that the internal host 10.20.2.17 attempted to access a Bit.ly shortened URL. The firewall blocked the connection because the website matched the organisation's blocked website policy. I then searched for additional activity from the same source IP address. I found another firewall event about two minutes earlier showing the same user performing a Google search for 'how to set up payroll system for small business'. This appeared to be normal browsing activity. I reviewed the available events around the same time and did not find any further suspicious connections or signs that the endpoint had been compromised.
4. Evidence and Analysis
The investigation was completed using Kibana only. I searched for events related to SourceIP 10.20.2.17 and compared the timestamps to build a timeline. The results showed one allowed Google search followed by one blocked request to a Bit.ly URL. This showed that the firewall successfully prevented access to a blacklisted website.

5. Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)
• Source IP: 10.20.2.17
• Destination IP: 67.199.248.11
• URL: http://bit.ly/3sHkX3da12340
• Protocol: TCP
• Destination Port: 80
• Firewall Rule: Blocked Websites
6. Verdict and Severity
Verdict: True Positive
Severity: Low

The alert is classified as a True Positive because the firewall detected and blocked an attempt to access a blacklisted website. Based on the Kibana logs, there was no evidence that the endpoint was compromised.

7. Recommendations and Remediation
• Continue monitoring the affected endpoint.
• Review browser history if available to identify how the user reached the Bit.ly link.
• Remind users not to click unknown or shortened links.
• Keep firewall block lists up to date.

8. Lessons Learned
This investigation showed the importance of reviewing events before and after an alert. Building a timeline using Kibana helped me understand what happened and supported my conclusion using evidence from the SIEM.

